# Chapter 11 — *Optimal Sleep*

**Nishi-Azabu. 9:40 p.m.**

The restaurant sat eight below street level, down a stair of wet black stone that somebody hosed every evening at six, and the room smelled of browned butter, burnt rosemary, and the faint green of the hinoki bar the chef had built himself. Four tables. One candle each, real wax, guttering in a thumb of oil. At the counter an older couple argued softly about a nephew's wedding. Behind them a salaryman on a date he hadn't earned laughed too loud, twice, then not at all when his companion checked her phone.

Minako Aino had been photographed in better light and never in kinder.

She'd chosen the dress for the stairs — matte black silk, cut on the bias so it moved a half-second behind her body, one thin strap, back open to the dimple above her ass. Nothing under it but a scrap of black lace that disappeared between her cheeks, the kind of thing she'd bought three of when a stylist told her panty lines were a career-limiting move. The silk lay over her tits without pretending to hold them; the room's air conditioning ran cold along the bar and her nipples had been standing under the fabric since the amuse-bouche, two clear points that the salaryman had found and lost and found again. Her hair was down, honey-thick, the red bow at the crown because she was still Minako and there were three phones in the room that might belong to somebody's cousin at a weekly. Her legs crossed under the table, calf hooked behind calf, one heel dangling off her toes.

Across from her, Ryō Sakaguchi was beautiful in charcoal and explaining a pivot table.

"So Hasegawa's the anchor," he said. He turned the phone toward her, thumb and forefinger widening the sheet until a column of green filled the screen. "Look. Four bookings since March, three of them repeat. That's a sixty-two percent recall rate, which — Minako, that's the number. Anything over fifty and you're not a face he *hired*, you're a face he *uses*. There's a difference and it's the whole difference."

"Sixty-two," she said, warm, and meant it warmly. He'd made the spreadsheet at his desk after hours. He'd color-coded it. Nobody had color-coded her before.

"Ozaki's the problem." He swiped. Yellow now. "One booking, no callback, and he shoots the Shiseido ancillary campaigns, which is where the drama people actually look. So we solve Ozaki." He said *we* the way he always said it, and it was a good word, she reminded herself, a word men in her life had historically failed to locate. "I ran the last eighteen months of his credits against agency rosters. He recasts inside his own comfort pool about eighty percent of the time, so the entry cost is high, but — here — his assistant left to go freelance in June, and the new one came from Sugimoto's studio, and *you* shot with Sugimoto in January."

"I did."

"So there's a path." He pinched the screen wider. "Second tab. I built out the drama side. This is Fuji's ten o'clock slot next season, they're casting the hospital one, and there are usually four to six speaking bits under three lines that go to talent-division faces. Historically eleven percent of those go to models with no prior credit. Eleven's low. Eleven's not zero."

The candle leaned. Somebody at the counter dropped a spoon and the chef didn't look up.

*He is kind,* she thought, with the deliberate evenness of a woman shelving something. *He has never once been late. He remembers that I hate coriander and he asks about my mother, and last month he drove to Saitama in the rain because I said the word 'lonely' in a text, and when he got there he brought soup and a phone charger, because he is a man who thinks about phone chargers.*

"Eleven percent," she said. "That's better than my odds at the claw machine."

"It's not really comparable, the claw machines are calibrated against —" He caught himself, and smiled, and it was a good smile, straight and slightly shy at one corner, the reason she'd said yes in the first place in a stairwell at the agency five months ago. "Sorry. Yes. Better than the claw machine."

She ate a piece of duck. It was very good duck. The skin cracked under the edge of her fork like a thin sheet of ice on a puddle, and the fig thing under it was black and sticky and she wanted to lick her thumb and couldn't, in that dress, in that room.

He went back to the phone.

"Third tab is just cost. Because if we're going after Ozaki we're eating a portfolio refresh, and if we're eating a refresh we should do it before the autumn cycle, which means booking a studio in the next —"

There was a version of this she could have loved. She knew it and turned it over and looked at it from underneath, the way she'd once looked at a rival's guard, hunting the gap. He was *building* something. He was building it for her, in nine-point type, on his own time, and there were women who would have wept over that spreadsheet.

Her thighs pressed together under the tablecloth and she thought about the sea.

Kaito, two boyfriends ago, had woken her at three in the morning in February to say *come look at the water*, and they'd driven out past Chiba with the heater broken and her bare feet on the dash, and he'd run the tank dry outside a shuttered noodle place and they'd sat on the hood until the sky went gray-pink and she'd laughed until her ribs hurt. Then she'd found out he'd been saying *come look at the water* to somebody in Yokohama. That was the trade, apparently. Men who made her feel like a lit match burned everything, including her, and men who kept her safe kept her in a cell with very good ventilation.

*I chose this one,* she reminded herself, reasonably. *I chose him because he's steady and he's easy to look at and because for once I wanted to come home to the same apartment twice in a row.*

"— and if Hasegawa's doing the winter editorial, the recall rate goes up again, which compounds, which is the part people miss about compounding —"

An alarm went off on his phone. Three ascending chimes, cheerful, institutional.

"Ah." He was already standing. He had the napkin off his lap and folded into a rectangle beside his plate in one movement. "That's Nakamura, he calls at quarter to on Tuesdays and if I don't take it it rolls to Wednesday and Wednesday's the roster meeting. Two minutes."

"Take it."

"Two minutes," he said again, apologetic, sincere, and went up the black stairs with the phone already at his ear and his shoulders squared like a man walking into weather.

---

The chair across from her held nobody. The candle stopped leaning.

Minako let her spine go soft against the chair back, which she never did in public, and slid her own phone out of the little bag. The screen lit her face blue-white from below. In the mirrored strip behind the bar she caught herself — mouth slack, eyes flat, the expression that lived under all the others — and put it away out of habit.

The contact had no surname. She'd typed it herself under a gas station canopy eleven days ago, standing in her stocking feet on cold concrete with her heels hooked over two fingers, while a stranger the size of a door bled through a butterfly closure and told her to stop fussing.

**BYL — REPAIRMAN**

She hadn't touched it since.

What came back to her came back whole, the way trained things do. The alley off the machine-parts district. Five men going in after one. The wet sound a pallet makes when a man's shoulder meets it. Lucy's low, businesslike growl around a forearm, no hysteria in it at all, a dog doing a job she'd been taught before Minako was born. And him, bleeding from the scalp into his eye, keeping his weight on his back foot, putting the skip between himself and three of them because the skip was the only thing in that alley that couldn't be flanked.

Then the canopy light. His shirt off. That back.

*Somebody taught you that,* he'd said, about her wrist lock, and he'd let it lie when she lied. Nobody let her lie. Everybody in her life either believed her or caught her, and he had done the third thing, the thing that had no name, and then he'd handed her his own phone so she could type.

She held the screen a moment with her thumb hovering.

*It's follow-up.* The scalp split had taken four butterflies and he'd refused stitches, and scalp wounds opened again when men were stupid, and he was a man who was stupid on purpose, professionally. *That's all this is. Medical due diligence. I'd text a teammate.* She had never once in her life texted a teammate.

**MINAKO (9:51 p.m.):** Scalp check. Did the butterflies hold or did you rip them off like an idiot on day two

The three dots came up, went away, and stayed away long enough that her stomach did something she chose not to examine. Two minutes. Three. The couple at the counter settled the nephew question. Then:

**BYL (9:54 p.m.):** day three. and they came off on their own, on a duct
**BYL:** so technically the duct ripped them off. I've got witnesses
**MINAKO:** Who, the duct
**BYL:** the duct and a dog with no legal standing
**MINAKO:** Is it scarring
**BYL:** everything scars. it's under the hair, nobody's paying to look at my scalp
**MINAKO:** Rude. I looked at it for free
**BYL:** you looked at it with tweezers and a lot of opinions

She laughed — out loud, short, one syllable — and the chef glanced up from the pass for the first time all evening.

**MINAKO:** I have excellent opinions. Where are you, it sounds loud
**BYL:** stairwell of a dead office building in Yoyogi. water heater died and took a section of ceiling with it out of spite
**BYL:** it's 10pm and I'm holding a bucket
**MINAKO:** Glamorous
**BYL:** the bucket's the glamorous part. the bucket has a future
**MINAKO:** What's Lucy doing
**BYL:** asleep on my jacket. she found the only dry square meter in the building in about four seconds and hasn't shared it
**MINAKO:** Smart girl
**BYL:** smartest one in the building. low bar tonight

It went like that for another four minutes, easy as breathing, the kind of back-and-forth that in her experience required either six months or alcohol, and she realized she'd been sitting with her elbow on the table and her chin in her hand like a teenager, in a two-hundred-thousand-yen dress, in the good light.

Footsteps on the black stairs.

She locked the phone face-down beside her water glass, unhurried, and had her spine straight and her ankles crossed before Ryō was halfway to the table.

---

"Sorry. Sorry. Roster's a mess, Kuroda's pulling two people off the variety block —" He sat, shook his napkin out, laid it across his lap. "Where were we. The refresh."

"Let's get ice cream."

He looked up. "Now?"

"After. There's a place in Azabu-Jūban that does black sesame and the guy scoops it like he's mad at it. It's open till one." She leaned in over the candle. The silk shifted and she felt his eyes drop once and come back up like a man returning a library book on time. "We walk. Twenty minutes. It's a nice night."

"Ah." He smiled, regretful, and checked the corner of his phone. "Eleven-twenty. I'm at eleven-twenty tonight."

"Eleven-twenty."

"Six-forty alarm. If I'm down by eleven-twenty I get five full cycles, and the fifth is where the actual consolidation happens, which is what makes Wednesday work. If I'm at four cycles I'm useless in the roster meeting and Kuroda notices." He said it kindly. That was somehow the part. "Saturday. We'll do ice cream Saturday. I'll put it in."

Minako put her chin down and looked up at him from under her lashes, and let her lower lip go heavy, the face that had sold a phone plan to half the Kanto region.

"That's not going to work on me," Ryō said. He laughed a little. "And — honestly, Minako, that face, at dinner, in a place like this. Somebody in here is going to have a camera. You're the one who tells me about the camera."

The candle burned. The salaryman at the counter paid.

"You're right," she said.

They split the last of the wine. He talked about Kuroda and the variety block and she said *mm* four times and *that's so typical of him* once, and the chef sent out two spoons of a black fig sorbet that tasted like a cellar, and she ate hers and then, when Ryō pushed his aside because of the sugar, she ate most of his.

Outside, the street was wet without having rained. He held her coat for her, which she did like, and put his hand on the small of her bare back going up the stairs, which she also liked, warm palm, right at the base of her spine.

"There's time," he said at the top, checking the phone corner again. "Eleven-oh-five. If we go to yours we've got twenty minutes and then I'll —" He tipped his head toward the taxi rank, and his thumb moved once over her spine, and there it was, the whole offer, scheduled and delivered on time.

"I've got a headache," Minako said.

A beat.

"You've had a lot of headaches."

She turned her head and looked at him, and whatever he found in her face made him take a half step back and check his phone corner for the third time in a minute.

"I didn't mean —" He kissed her cheek. His lips were dry and warm and he smelled like the cedar cologne she'd picked out for him in March. "Saturday. Ice cream. I'll put it in the calendar, I'll actually put it in. Text me when you're home safe."

He got into the second taxi at the rank because the first one's driver was smoking, and the door swung shut, and the little white car took him away toward five full cycles.

---

Minako stood on the wet street in her black silk with her arms crossed under her tits and the bow slipping loose at the crown of her head, and counted to ten.

Then she had the phone out.

**MINAKO (11:09 p.m.):** I'm bored
**MINAKO:** Extremely bored. Catastrophically. It's a public health situation
**MINAKO:** You're going to come meet me and be interesting at me for an hour. That's the price of the butterflies
**MINAKO:** Bring the dog. The dog is the main draw, I want that clear

The dots came up almost at once.

**BYL (11:10 p.m.):** I'm covered in ceiling
**MINAKO:** Perfect. Wear it
**BYL:** where

She already knew. Nine floors of grubby office building three blocks off the park, where she'd shot a tights campaign in July, sweating through eleven setups on a rooftop while a man with a reflector told her to think about *freedom*. The stairwell door up there had a latch that hadn't caught since before she was born; the building manager had complained about it on camera, twice, loudly, and everyone had ignored him. From the parapet you could see the whole city lie down and glitter — the park a black hole in the middle of it, the towers of Shinjuku standing up out of the dark like something waiting to be asked a question.

**MINAKO:** Yoyogi. The Sanwa building, the ugly brown one by the pachinko place. Roof.
**MINAKO:** Door doesn't lock. Don't tell anyone, it's my only crime
**BYL:** that's four blocks from the bucket
**MINAKO:** Then you have no excuse
**BYL:** I have eleven excuses. give me twenty minutes

She dropped the phone in her bag, stepped out of the light of the restaurant stair, and hailed the first taxi with the smoking driver, who took one look at her and put it out. In the back seat she pulled the bow out of her hair and shook it loose over her shoulders, and thumbed a message to Ryō — *Home safe, sleep well* — sent from a car going the wrong direction entirely, and put her bare feet up out of the heels, and grinned at the black glass with her teeth showing.
